  "slug": "mapping-glycosylation-prokaryotes-01b0",
  "summary": "Open-source workflows and plugins now infer microbial function and networks directly from 16S rRNA k-mers, compositional data, and shotgun metagenomes.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is computational tooling for microbiome data, spanning reproducible workflows and statistical or machine-learning inference. q2-classo and q2-gglasso add sparse log-contrast regression, classification, and microbial network estimation on compositional data as QIIME 2 plugins. OpusTaxa is an open-source Snakemake workflow that chains host removal, taxonomic profiling, assembly, and functional analysis for shotgun metagenomes. embeRNA takes a different route, predicting microbial functions directly from 16S rRNA k-mer embeddings and is reported to outperform reference-based methods on novel microbes. What is new across these items is inference that reduces dependence on reference-based steps: sparse compositional modelling in the QIIME 2 plugins and sequence-composition-based function prediction in embeRNA. The items do not address a single shared biological question.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-04-22"
}
